  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA moisture-starved cold front crosses the area this morning, then \nhigh pressure swiftly regains control and allows the ongoing dry \nspell to extend into Saturday. \n\nBoth today and Friday, afternoon afternoon high temperatures are \nexpected to be in the upper 50s to low 70s in the mountains and 70s \nin the lowlands. Dry air will keep dewpoints much lower, causing \nrelative humidity values to lower into the 30 to 40 percent range in \nthe mountains and upper 20s to 30s for much of the lowlands each \nafternoon. While winds should remain light on Friday, breezy \nconditions are expected to develop this afternoon. Some gusts to 20 \nmph could be possible in the lowlands, but best potential for \nstronger gusts, to 30 mph, will be in the northeast mountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe remnants of Isaias are expected to lift into the Ohio Valley \nthis weekend, with precipitation chances increasing as tropical \nmoisture invades the forecast area late Saturday afternoon into \nSaturday night. Widespread rain should then persist as a shortwave \ntrough ushers the weakening system east across the area Sunday and \nSunday night. \n\nModels generally depict rainfall amounts of one to two inches across \nthe majority of the forecast area through Sunday night, though \nvariations in the timing and track of the system could result in \nhigher accumulations. At least a low risk of flooding is expected to \ndevelop beneath bands of heavier rain and in poor-drainage areas. \n\nWhile the risk of severe weather should be low, can't completely \nrule out the possibility of a few stronger storms. Also, outside of \nstorms, winds may gust into the 20 to 40 mph range at times, with \nstrongest gusts occurring along the mountains Saturday night and \nSunday.\n\nPrecipitation tapers off and drier conditions briefly take hold as \nthe weakening system heads into the Atlantic on Monday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA low-pressure system, which passes well to the north, is projected \nto steer a cold front across the area Tuesday into Tuesday night. \nHigh pressure and an upper ridge then transit the area and support \nanother brief dry spell mid-week.",
